" (https://lhote.users.greyc.fr/articles/Issac2016.pdf)\nciteturn9894view0 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: 3 weeks ago; Content type: application/pdf; Number of pages: 8; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://lhote.users.greyc.fr/articles/Issac2016.pdf\",\"lineno\":null}); Total lines: 1064\nL0@P0: Analysis of the Brun Gcd Algorithm\nL1@P0: Valérie Berthé\nL2@P0: IRIF (CNRS and Université\nL3@P0: Paris Diderot), France\nL4@P0: berthe@liafa.univ-paris\u0002diderot.fr\nL5@P0: Loïck Lhote\nL6@P0: GREYC (CNRS, ENSICAEN,\nL7@P0: and Université of Caen),\nL8@P0: France\nL9@P0: loick.lhote@ensicaen.fr\nL10@P0: Brigitte Vallée\nL11@P0: GREYC (CNRS, ENSICAEN,\nL12@P0: and Université of Caen),\nL13@P0: France\nL14@P0: brigitte.vallee@unicaen.fr\nL15@P0: ABSTRACT\nL16@P0: We introduce and study a multiple gcd algorithm that is\nL17@P0: a natural extension of the usual Euclid algorithm, and co\u0002incides with it for two entries; it performs Euclidean divi\u0002sions, between the largest entry and the second largest en\u0002try, and then re-orderings. This is the discrete version of a\nL18@P0: multidimensional continued fraction algorithm due to Brun.\nL19@P0: We perform the average-case analysis of this algorithm, and\nL20@P0: prove that the mean number of steps is linear with respect\nL21@P0: to the size of the entry. The method relies on dynamical\nL22@P0: analysis, and is based on the study of the underlying Brun\nL23@P0: dynamical system. The dominant constant of the analysis\nL24@P0: is related to the entropy of the system. We also compare\nL25@P0: this algorithm to another extension of the Euclid algorithm,\nL26@P0: proposed by Knuth, and already analyzed by the authors.\nL27@P0: Keywords\nL28@P0: GCD algorithms; Brun continued fractions; average-case ana\u0002lysis; analytic combinatorics; Dirichlet generating functions;\nL29@P0: dynamical analysis; transfer operators; Tauberian theorems\nL30@P0: Acknowledgments. Thanks to the ANR DynA3S (ANR\u000213-BS02-0003) and AleaEnAmSud AmSud-STIC Projects.\nL31@P0: 1. INTRODUCTION.\nL32@P0: General Context. We study a multiple gcd algorithm\nL33@P0: that is a natural extension of the usual Euclid algorithm\nL34@P0: for (d + 1) integers, and coincides with it for d = 1. This is\nL35@P0: a discrete version of a multidimensional continued fraction\nL36@P0: algorithm, that is itself based on a dynamical system, the\nL37@P0: Brun dynamical system, described for instance in [6, 12].\nL38@P0: The Brun continued fraction algorithm admits various de\u0002scriptions and appears under various names; it is closely re\u0002lated to the Podsypanin modified Jacobi–Perron algorithm,\nL39@P0: it is also called the d-dimensional Gauss transformation or\nL40@P0: the ordered Jacobi–Perron algorithm [7].\nL41@P0: This dynamical system belongs to the class of multidimen\u0002sional unimodular continued fraction algorithms, described\nL42@P0: Permission to make digital or hard copies of all or part of this work for personal or\nL43@P0: classroom use is granted without fee provided that copies are not made or distributed\nL44@P0: for profit or commercial advantage and that copies bear this notice and the full cita\u0002tion on the first page. Copyrights for components of this work owned by others than\nL45@P0: ACM must be honored. Abstracting with credit is permitted. To copy otherwise, or re\u0002publish, to post on servers or to redistribute to lists, requires prior specific permission\nL46@P0: and/or a fee. Request permissions from permissions@acm.org.\nL47@P0: ISSAC ’16, July 19–22, 2016, Waterloo, ON, Canada.\nL48@P0: \nL49@P0: c 2016 ACM. ISBN 978-1-4503-4380-0/16/07. . . $15.00\nL50@P0: DOI: http://dx.doi.org/10.1145/2930889.2930899\nL51@P0: in [12], which produce simultaneous diophantine approxima\u0002tions of a real vector. Then, the literature, for instance in\nL52@P0: [9, 5], mainly focuses on the convergence of these approxi\u0002mations, closely related to the Lyapunov exponents of the\nL53@P0: underlying dynamical systems. These algorithms have also\nL54@P0: important applications to discrete geometry [3].\nL55@P0: With each multidimensional continued fraction algorithm, a\nL56@P0: gcd algorithm may be of course associated. However, the\nL57@P0: analysis of such a class of gcd algorithms has not been yet\nL58@P0: considered. This is our general project, and we begin by the\nL59@P0: BrunGcd Algorithm, as it is one of the most “natural” algo\u0002rithms of this class, and shares many important properties\nL60@P0: with the Euclid dynamical system based on the Gauss map.\nL61@P0: Main results. We perform the probabilistic analysis of the\nL62@P0: BrunGcd algorithm. We first focus on the total number of\nL63@P0: steps and prove that it is on average linear in the size of the\nL64@P0: entries. The dominant constant equals (d + 1)/Ed where Ed\nL65@P0: is the entropy of the Brun dynamical system. This entropy\nL66@P0: is not precisely studied in the literature, but there exists\nL67@P0: a conjecture in [7] that states that Ed is Θ(1) for d → ∞.\nL68@P0: However, we show that Ed is ∼ log d for d → ∞, so that the\nL69@P0: dominant constant for the number of steps grows as d/ log d.\nL70@P0: We then compare the BrunGcd with another multiple gcd al\u0002gorithm, the PlainGcd algorithm, described in Knuth’s book\nL71@P0: [8], which deals with the classical one-dimensional Euclid\nL72@P0: dynamical system. The authors have already analyzed this\nL73@P0: algorithm in [4], and prove that the mean number of steps is\nL74@P0: also linear in the size of the entries. However, the dominant\nL75@P0: constant is independent of the dimension d and equals 2/E1,\nL76@P0: where E1 is the entropy of the Euclid dynamical system. We\nL77@P0: conclude that the PlainGcd algorithm is much more efficient\nL78@P0: than the BrunGcd algorithm, in particular for large d.\nL79@P0: We finally explain the inefficiency of the BrunGcd algorithm\nL80@P0: when d is large: almost all divisions deal with a quotient\nL81@P0: equal to 1. Then the main operation performed is not a di\u0002vision but... a plain subtraction. This is reinforced by the\nL82@P0: comparison with the subtractive version of the algorithm,\nL83@P0: whose number of steps is proven to be also of linear complex\u0002ity. This exhibits two strong differences with the classical\nL84@P0: Gcd algorithm (case d = 1).\nL85@P0: Methods. We use here the methods of dynamical anal\u0002ysis such as developed in [2, 10, 13]: a gcd algorithm is\nL86@P0: viewed as a dynamical system, with each iterative step be\u0002ing a linear fractional transformation. Costs of interest are\nL87@P0: then described with Dirichlet generating functions that are\nL88@P0: algebraically related to transfer operators of the system. The\nL89@P0: main analytical property of these series is the existence of a\nL90@P0-1: dominant pole, which is itself closely related to the existenceof a spectral gap for the corresponding transfer operators.\nL91@P1: The asymptotic extraction of coefficients is then achieved by\nL92@P1: means of Tauberian theorems.\nL93@P1: Plan of the paper. We first introduce the algorithm in\nL94@P1: Section 2 and state the main results. Then, we study in Sec\u0002tion 3 the underlying dynamical system, closely related to\nL95@P1: the Brun dynamical system, and we provide a characteriza\u0002tion of its rational trajectories. The following two sections\nL96@P1: perform the dynamical analysis of the algorithm, with its\nL97@P1: two steps, the combinatorial step (Section 4) and the ana\u0002lytic step (Section 5). The paper ends with open problems.\nL98@P1: 2. THE BRUN GCD ALGORITHM.\nL99@P1: We describe the BrunGcd algorithm, state the main complex\u0002ity results, and compare it with the PlainGcd algorithm.\nL100@P1: 2.1 General description.\nL101@P1: The algorithm BrunGcd(d) computes the gcd of (d+ 1) posi\u0002tive integers. It deals with the input set Ω(d) which gathers\nL102@P1: the ordered (d+ 1)-uples u formed with positive and distinct\nL103@P1: integer numbers\nL104@P1: Ω(d) := {u = (u0, u1, . . . , ud) | u0 > u1 > u2 > . . . > ud > 0} .\nL105@P1: During the execution of the algorithm, some components\nL106@P1: “disappear” and the algorithm deals with the disjoint union\nL107@P1: Γ(d) =\nL108@P1: Md−1\nL109@P1: `=0\nL110@P1: Ω(d−`) .\nL111@P1: The algorithm BrunGcd(d) performs a sequence of steps, and\nL112@P1: each step deals with the pair (u0, u1) (that contains the two\nL113@P1: largest entries of u) and the list End u which gathers all the\nL114@P1: components of u except u0; it divides the first component\nL115@P1: u0 by the second component u1, and creates a remainder v0\nL116@P1: v0 := u0 − mu1, m := \u0014\nL117@P1: u0\nL118@P1: u1\nL119@P1: \u0015\nL120@P1: .\nL121@P1: Then, the procedure InsDis (v0, End u) inserts v0 ≥ 0 at a\nL122@P1: suitable position inside the list End u, so that the result re\u0002mains an ordered uple of distinct positive values: the second\nL123@P1: component u1 becomes the largest one, and there are three\nL124@P1: possible cases for the insertion (or not) of v0:\nL125@P1: (G) (Generic case) if v0 is not present in the list End u, this\nL126@P1: is a usual insertion;\nL127@P1: (Z) (Zero case) if v0 = 0, we do not insert v0;\nL128@P1: (E) (Equality case) if v0 6= 0 is already present in the list\nL129@P1: End u at position i, we do not insert v0.\nL130@P1: In each of the cases (Z) or (E), we do not insert v0, but\nL131@P1: we memorize the potential insertion position (in case (E),\nL132@P1: we would have inserted v0 “in front of” ui). Finally, each\nL133@P1: step of the algorithm BrunGcd(d) is described by the map\nL134@P1: U(d) : Γ(d) → Γ(d) which associates with u\nL135@P1: U(d)(u) = InsDis (u0 mod u1, End u). (1)\nL136@P1: The algorithm BrunGcd(d) described in the following figure\nL137@P1: decomposes into d phases, labelled from ` = 0 to ` = d − 1.\nL138@P1: During each phase, a component is “lost”, and the `-th phase,\nL139@P1: denoted